  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Concourse Departure Board",
    "overview": "Thermals is a rail concoursedeparture board where sensors, cores and processes are departures. Readings use matte split-flap cells inside black housings mounted on a steel frame. Barlow Condensed capitals carry measurements and labels; short mixed-case notes explain units, source limitations and recovery. All history charts use the full blue-to-red ramp to show magnitude. CPU/RAM history colors do not assert OS pressure. Other utilization readings use the blue-to-pale-sodium portion; explicit pressure labels communicate warnings.\n\nThe board is dense and ordered the way a real departures board is. Rows hold their position (sensors by natural order of their board label, cores P1.. then E1..) so the eye can return to the same slot; only the user's processes are ranked, and they slide to their new rank rather than jumping. The popover is a fixed 400pt wide and each board uses its natural height, capped by the current screen’s visible height minus 32pt. Oversized host proposals must not stretch it. Only genuine overflow scrolls, with tabs and footer kept reachable. The board is always rendered dark, regardless of system appearance; only the menu bar reading adapts to a light scheme.\n\nThis world was chosen by the owner over a liquid-crystal strip. It refuses the stock stats popover (gauges, rings, system-gray list rows) and refuses gamer RGB: no rainbow cycling, no neon, no glow beyond a lit lamp.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Split-flap cells for every value; steel column labels name the data.",
      "One shared palette with distinct temperature, utilization and OS-pressure meanings.",
      "Fixed row order; only processes rank, and they animate to their new place.",
      "CPU temperature reference bands use COOL, WARM, HOT and BURN; utilization uses LOW, MOD and HIGH.",
      "Steel frame, black housings, matte flaps; no cards, no translucency, no system materials inside the board."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Gradient Rule",
        "body": "Reading colors come from Board. All history charts use `heat`, with the full palette representing magnitude on their fixed axes. CPU/RAM history red means high utilization, not a pressure alarm. Other utilization readings use `usage` (the first 55% of the ramp); pressure labels retain their explicit warning semantics.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Derived Is Neutral Rule",
        "body": "An aggregate the app derives rather than measures (UNMEASURED EST) is shown in steel light with an unlit lamp and an empty STATUS. It is never banded with per-process thresholds.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Unscaled Is Neutral Rule",
        "body": "A reading with no scale shared across Macs (system POWER and GPU in watts: an Air peaks near 20 W, a Max past 100 W) is inked letter white, never placed on the heat gradient, since the same watts would mean different heat on different chips.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Always Colored Rule",
        "body": "Menu-bar values remain non-template colored images in both appearances. OS serious/critical pressure adds an explicit warning in every menu mode and raises temperature ink even when the temperature is unavailable. A stale selected source is withheld and marked with a question mark.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Pressure Is A State Rule",
        "body": "Thermal and memory pressure are discrete OS states, not readings, so they use fixed inks, not the gradient: thermal NOMINAL steel light, FAIR letter white, SERIOUS amber, CRITICAL burn red; memory NORMAL steel light, WARN amber, CRITICAL burn red.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Cell Is The Unit Rule",
        "body": "Measurements use fixed flap cells, uppercase and right-aligned; names are left-aligned. Truncated names end in an ellipsis and expose their full text on hover and to accessibility. Numeric overflow shows hashes, never a smaller-looking prefix; fan RPM has five cells for the supported 20,000 RPM maximum.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Sizes Rule",
        "body": "Flap text comes in exactly two sizes, hero and row. Hierarchy beyond that comes from position and heat color, not new sizes.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Fixed Platform Rule",
        "body": "Measured rows keep a fixed order: sensors by board label, cores P1.. then E1.., memory parts APP to FREE. Rows never re-sort by value; ranked rows that jump were rejected.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Still Footer Rule",
        "body": "The shell fits the selected content instead of measuring every board. Only content that exceeds the available screen height scrolls; navigation, updates and footer remain reachable. No blank filler rows or nested sensor/app scrolling. Changing boards or opening Settings resets the content scroll position and stops irrelevant process/footprint sampling.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Housing Not Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Separation is a black housing or a panel with a 1pt steel-mid hairline at 45%; never a drop shadow, blur or vibrancy material inside the board.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do use `Board.heat` for temperature and `Board.usage` for utilization, with OS pressure kept explicit.",
      "Do set board values in flap strips at hero (38 x 58pt) or row (11.5 x 19pt) size, uppercase, numbers right-aligned.",
      "Do keep measured rows ordered and show the complete selected board whenever it fits; scroll only for screen overflow.",
      "Do reserve heat words for CPU temperature reference bands; use utilization words for CPU and memory.",
      "Do honor Reduce Motion: flips swap instantly and ranked rows stop animating.",
      "Do show placeholders (--, UNAVAILABLE, SAMPLING, COLLECTING READINGS) in steel light rather than a guessed value."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use gauges, rings or system-gray list rows; the board is flaps, lamps and lenses.",
      "Don't use gamer RGB: no rainbow cycling, neon, or glow beyond a lit lamp.",
      "Don't step colors per band or hand-pick a band color; the gradient is continuous within bands.",
      "Don't sort measured rows by value or let rows jump; only processes rank, and they slide.",
      "Don't band a derived aggregate; UNMEASURED EST stays neutral steel.",
      "Don't let the menu bar reading fall back to a monochrome template.",
      "Don't add drop shadows, blur or vibrancy inside the board."
    ]
  }
